    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d46">

                <pb facs="0153" n="41" xml:id="pg41"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/6856: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-04T19:00:00+09:00">February 4, 1935—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 4—7:58 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>23. On January 30 and February 3 the Tokyo correspondent of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi> telegraphed analyses of the
                    Sino-Japanese situation that merit attention. I am aware that he has consulted
                    high Foreign Office and other officials as well as businessmen. His information
                    is usually accurate, although in this case he seems not to stress sufficiently
                    the economic aspects of the situation, which may prove to be the determining
                    factor in any arrangement reached in the present conversations.</p>
                <p>The Foreign Office here continues to state that Japan is making no demands on
                    China at the present time.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
